-- title:
--   Existence of a uniform long-edge path system for every bounded configuration
-- statement:
--   Let $d \ge 1$, let $\vartheta$ satisfy $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$, and let $R_0 \in \mathbb{R}$ be arbitrary. Then there exist positive integers $N, M$ and a real $\lambda$ with $R_0 \le \lambda$ — depending only on $d$, $\vartheta$ and $R_0$, and in particular not on the configuration — such that for **every** $\vartheta$-bounded configuration $\Gamma$ on $\mathbb{R}^d$ (that is, every assignment $x \mapsto \Gamma(x)$ of a double cone with unit axis and apex angle at least $\vartheta$) the following holds. Writing $G(\Gamma)$ for the graph on the lattice $\mathbb{Z}^d$ in which distinct $x, y$ are joined when $y \in x + \Gamma(x)$ or $x \in y + \Gamma(y)$, there is a family of walks $p_{x,y}$ in $G(\Gamma)$, one for each ordered pair of lattice points and running from $x$ to $y$, such that
--
--   * each $p_{x,y}$ has at most $N$ edges;
--   * each unoriented edge of the lattice belongs to $p_{x,y}$ for at most $M$ pairs $(x,y)$;
--   * every edge $e$ of $p_{x,y}$ satisfies
--   $$\lambda^{-1}\|x-y\| \;\le\; |e| \;\le\; \lambda\,\|x-y\| \qquad\text{and}\qquad |e| > R_0 ,$$
--   where $|e|$ is the Euclidean length of the edge.
--
--   This is the chaining theorem in its discrete form: every pair of lattice points can be joined by a uniformly short walk whose edges are all comparable in length to the separation of the endpoints and are individually long, with a bounded-multiplicity guarantee that prevents any single edge from being overloaded. It is exactly the input a summation argument needs to compare a cone-restricted quadratic form with the full fractional energy, and the uniformity in $\Gamma$ is what makes the resulting constants depend only on $d$ and $\vartheta$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The threshold $R_0$ is an unconstrained real: it may be negative, in which case the clause $|e| > R_0$ is vacuous, and it may be arbitrarily large, in which case it is absorbed by the requirement $R_0 \le \lambda$ and the corresponding growth of the constants.
